; bridge_ir_ws2812.loom
;
; Decisions (2026-11-03): both threads spill into thread 2's quarter (339
; of 512 words), so thread 2 stays idle beside the bridge. The key codes
; are the widely published table for the common 21-key NEC remote, not a
; manufacturer's document. A held key steps on every repeat code.
;
; An infrared remote drives an LED strip, with no host in the loop: thread
; 0 decodes NEC frames and repeat codes from a receiver module on IN0 and
; turns each key into a strip state; thread 1 drives a WS2812B strip on
; OUT0 and refreshes it whenever that state changes. The host loads the
; image, sets thread 0's tick and starts both threads; nothing passes
; through the FIFOs afterwards.
;
; Written on 2026-11-03, after the hardware was complete (D-032), as a
; protocol pair Loom was not designed for (D-035). Nothing in Loom was built
; for infrared or for WS2812. Thread 0 is firmware/ir_nec_rx.loom's
; receiver: the timed waits (WAITP ... T against SETD, SEMANTICS 6.4, built
; for PS/2 and I2C timeouts) measure every mark and space, and CSRW
; TICK_INT restarts the tick grid on each accepted edge (SEMANTICS 4, built
; for the UART, CAN and Manchester receivers). Thread 1 is
; firmware/ws2812.loom's transmitter: every edge is a deadline-latched pin
; write (SETP pin, v, D, SEMANTICS 6.10, built for clock-exact edges). The
; two meet in one word of data memory (LD/ST, 6.11, built so device
; emulators can keep a register file). What the firmware does that no
; hardware does: NEC's pulse-distance decoding and checks, the command
; map, brightness scaling, the WS2812 bit timing and the hand-off.
;
; Pins: RX = IN0 (thread 0; the demodulated output of a TSOP38238 or alike,
; low while a 38 kHz burst is present, idle high), DOUT = OUT0 (thread 1; to
; the strip's DIN, through a 5 V level shifter on a real board).
;
; -------------------------------------------------------- the command map
; Standard NEC frames to address ADDR (0x00) only, with the key codes of
; the common 21-key NEC remote (QUESTIONS.md 1: taken from the widely
; published key table of that remote, not from a manufacturer's document):
;     0..9  0x16 0x0C 0x18 0x5E 0x08 0x1C 0x5A 0x42 0x52 0x4A
;           colour 0..9, and the strip on
;     VOL+  0x15  one level brighter (nothing when off or at level 7)
;     VOL-  0x07  one level dimmer (nothing when off or at level 0)
;     PLAY  0x43  on / off (colour and level are kept)
; A repeat code (the key is still held) applies the last frame's key again
; if that key was VOL+ or VOL-, so a held key ramps one level every 108 ms.
; Anything else ends the hold, and a repeat after it does nothing: a frame
; of another address, an extended (16-bit) address, a wrong inverse, a key
; not in the map, a mark or space out of its window. Such frames are
; ignored: they change nothing on the strip.
;
; Colours (R, G, B at level 7): 0 white 255 255 255, 1 red 255 0 0, 2
; orange 255 96 0, 3 yellow 255 224 0, 4 green 0 255 0, 5 cyan 0 255 255,
; 6 blue 0 0 255, 7 violet 128 0 255, 8 magenta 255 0 160, 9 warm white
; 255 160 64. At level L each byte is c >> (7 - L): a step halves or
; doubles the light, which the eye sees as even steps. After RUN the strip
; is off, colour 0, level 4, and the first frame blanks it.
;
; STATE (one word): bits 3:0 the colour, 6:4 the level, 7 on; initial 0x40.
;
; -------------------------------------------------------------- the strip
; Every LED shows the same colour, so a frame needs no buffer: thread 1
; holds G, R and B in three registers and sends them NLEDS times. NLEDS is
; a data word the host may patch before RUN (1 .. 65535; 0 is not allowed);
; it is 60 in the image, one metre of a 60 LED/m strip, a frame of 1.84 ms.
; It is bounded by the refresh time (30.72 us an LED) and the supply, not
; by the word budget. The tests use 8 on the RTL to save clocks.
;
; Timing as ws2812.loom (whose header gives the datasheet windows): TICK_INT
; 1, so every WAITD counts clocks at 50 MHz: T0H 20 clocks (0.40 us), T1H
; 40 (0.80), period 64 (1.28 us) for both values, so T0L 44 and T1L 24; the
; line then stays low RSTN * RSTW = 2750 clocks (55 us > 50 us) before the
; next frame can start. Thread 1 sets its own tick: the datasheet fixes the
; rate, so the host must not.
;
; The byte fetch. ws2812.loom's fetch from INQ is five slots; rotating G, R
; and B through r0 would be seven, two more than a 0 bit's low phase has.
; So the bit loop is written three times, one copy per byte of an LED: at
; the end of a G byte the G copy loads R (MOV, LDI) and jumps into the R
; copy, R loads B, and B counts the LED (DJNZ r6) and loads G or ends the
; frame. Every byte fetch is then two or three slots and no POP is left to
; declare bounded: every pair is proved (worst slack in the listing).
;
; ------------------------------------------- the hand-off (SEMANTICS 6.11)
; One shared word, STATE, written by thread 0 only (one ST) and read by
; thread 1 only (one LD). An LD or ST is one access at one edge, so the
; word is never half written. Thread 1 reads STATE once, computes G, R and
; B into registers before the first rise, and the whole frame comes from
; those registers; it reads again only after the frame and its reset gap.
; So every frame shows one state, never a mix of two, and a frame is never
; cut short. Changes faster than frames coalesce (the newest wins, and the
; strip always ends on the last state). Thread 1 compares each read with
; the state it last sent and starts a frame only when they differ, so the
; strip is refreshed only on a change: a key that changes nothing (a digit
; already shown, VOL+ at level 7) sends nothing. Thread 1 never waits on
; thread 0 (no flag, no FIFO), LD takes two of its own slots whatever the
; others do (6.11) and the barrel gives it every fourth clock (SEMANTICS
; 2), so its edges do not depend on the IR traffic. The latency from the
; end of a key's last burst to the first rise is at most one frame and its
; reset gap (if one is running) plus 5 us of polling and about 1 us of
; arithmetic.
;
; Shared pin registers: thread 0 only reads IN0 and writes none of the pin
; CSRs; thread 1 drives OUT0 with SETP only, and pin commits are
; bit-masked (SEMANTICS 6.3); neither touches OD_MASK, PIN_OE, OUTGRP or
; BE_PINS. No shared register is read-modify-written, so the start-up race
; of two threads each setting a bit of OD_MASK (W-037 hazard 2) cannot
; happen here.
;
; Memory, 339 of the 512 words: thread 0 151 words (0 .. 120 the
; receiver and the key lookup, 302 .. 331 apply), thread 1 142 words (128
; .. 240 the transmitter, 332 .. 360 change), the data 46 words at 256 ..
; 301 (STATE, NLEDS, LAST, the colours, the key map). Neither thread fits
; its quarter (the receiver alone is 112 words, the three bit loops 75), so
; both spill into thread 2's quarter after the data: thread 2 cannot run
; beside the bridge, and thread 3's quarter (384 .. 511) is free for a
; third program.

        .imem   512                     ; the macro build: quarters of 128 words
        .pins   RX = IN0, DOUT = OUT0
        .equ    DATA  = 256             ; STATE, NLEDS, LAST, colours, keys
        .equ    COLS  = DATA + 3        ; colour c: G, R, B at COLS + 3c .. + 2
        .equ    KEYS  = COLS + 30       ; the key map: action << 8 | code
        .equ    NKEYS = 13
        .equ    ADDR  = 0x00            ; the remote's address
        .equ    UP    = 10              ; actions: 0..9 a colour, then these
        .equ    DOWN  = 11
        .equ    POWER = 12
        .equ    NONE  = 63              ; LAST: no key to repeat
        .equ    ON    = 0x80            ; STATE bit 7

; ================================================= thread 0: NEC to STATE
; ir_nec_rx.loom's receiver (its header gives the tick, the windows, the
; glitch filter and how a pulse is measured), with its reports replaced:
; a good frame or a repeat code applies the command map to STATE, and
; every error (a window, a timeout, an inverse) ends the hold instead of
; being pushed. The tick comes from the host: 1757 + 208/256 clocks at
; 50 MHz, 1/16 of the NEC unit; 64 or more (the receiver's header).
; r0 the item, then the command; r1 = 0x8000; r2 the address half, then
; the action; r3 the command half, then STATE; r4 bits left, then scratch;
; r5 the glitch-filter delay; r6 TICK_INT; r7 scratch and the data base.
        .thread 0
        .tick   64                      ; TICK_INT 64 or more (header)
        .equ    G     = 2               ; glitch filter, ticks
        .equ    LMMIN = 188 - G         ; leader mark window, minus the filter
        .equ    LMMAX = 324 - G
        .equ    RSMIN = 44 - G          ; repeat space
        .equ    RSMAX = 84 - G
        .equ    LSMIN = 92 - G          ; leader space
        .equ    LSMAX = 164 - G
        .equ    MMIN  = 8 - G           ; a one-unit mark or space
        .equ    MMAX  = 24 - G
        .equ    S1MIN = 32 - G          ; the space of a 1
        .equ    S1MAX = 64 - G
        .equ    I_REP = 16              ; r0: measuring a repeat code

start:  CSRR    r6, TICK_INT            ; P, kept for the restarts
        MOV     r5, r6
        SHRI    r5, 1                   ; P / 2 slots = 2 ticks: the glitch filter,
        SUBI    r5, 4                   ; less the 4 other slots of wlow / whigh
        LDI     r1, 0
        LDIH    r1, 0x80                ; r1 = 0x8000

; Hunt for a leader: an idle (high) line first, then a confirmed mark.
hunt:   WAITP   RX, 1
h2:     SETD    255                     ; re-armed every 255 ticks
        CALL    wlow
        BT      h2                      ; no mark yet
        LDI     r4, 32
        LDI     r0, 0
        SETD    LMMIN                   ; the leader mark, 188 .. 324 ticks
        CALL    whigh
        BNT     hunt                    ; too short for a leader: noise, ignored
        SETD    LMMAX - LMMIN
        CALL    whigh
        BT      bad                     ; too long

; The leader space: 44 .. 84 ticks is a repeat code, 92 .. 164 a frame.
        SETD    RSMIN
        CALL    wlow
        BNT     bad                     ; too short
        SETD    RSMAX - RSMIN
        CALL    wlow
        BNT     rep                     ; a repeat code
        SETD    LSMIN - RSMAX
        CALL    wlow
        BNT     bad                     ; between the two windows
        SETD    LSMAX - LSMIN
        CALL    wlow
        BT      bad                     ; too long
        JMP     mark                    ; a frame: 32 data bits follow
rep:    LDI     r0, I_REP               ; a repeat code: one burst follows
        LDI     r4, 0

; A mark, 8 .. 24 ticks; r4 = data bits left (0: the last burst).
mark:   SETD    MMIN
        CALL    whigh
        BNT     bad                     ; too short
        SETD    MMAX - MMIN
        CALL    whigh
        BT      bad                     ; too long
        TEST    r4, r4
        BZ      done                    ; that was the last burst

; A space: 8 .. 24 ticks is a 0, 32 .. 64 a 1, least significant first.
        SETD    MMIN
        CALL    wlow
        BNT     bad                     ; too short
        SETD    MMAX - MMIN
        CALL    wlow
        BNT     zero                    ; a 0
        SETD    S1MIN - MMAX
        CALL    wlow
        BNT     bad                     ; between the 0 and 1 windows
        SETD    S1MAX - S1MIN
        CALL    wlow
        BT      bad                     ; too long: the frame stopped
        SHRI    r3, 1
        OR      r3, r3, r1              ; a 1
        JMP     next
zero:   SHRI    r3, 1
next:   DEC     r4
        CMPI    r4, 16
        BNZ     mark
        MOV     r2, r3                  ; the first 16 bits are whole
        JMP     mark

; The last burst has ended. A repeat code applies LAST if it is VOL+ or
; VOL-; a frame must be standard NEC to ADDR with a good inverse.
done:   MOV16   r7, DATA
        TEST    r0, r0
        BZ      frame
        LD      r2, r7, 2               ; LAST
        CMPI    r2, UP
        BNC     hunt                    ; a colour: not repeated
        CMPI    r2, POWER
        BC      hunt                    ; POWER or NONE: not repeated
        JMP     apply
frame:  MOV     r4, r3                  ; the command half: ~command, command
        SWAP    r4, r4
        XOR     r4, r4, r3
        NOT     r4, r4                  ; zero when bits 15:8 invert bits 7:0
        BNZ     bad
        MOV     r4, r2                  ; the same test on the address half
        SWAP    r4, r4
        XOR     r4, r4, r2
        NOT     r4, r4
        BNZ     bad                     ; an extended (16-bit) address
        LDI     r4, ADDR
        XOR     r4, r4, r2
        SHLI    r4, 8                   ; Z: the address is ADDR
        BNZ     bad
        MOV     r0, r3
        SHLI    r0, 8
        SHRI    r0, 8                   ; the command
        MOV16   r7, KEYS
        LDI     r4, NKEYS
look:   LD      r2, r7, 0               ; action << 8 | code
        XOR     r3, r2, r0
        SHLI    r3, 8                   ; Z: this key
        BZ      found
        INC     r7
        DJNZ    r4, look
bad:    MOV16   r2, NONE << 8           ; not for us: ignored, the hold ends
found:  SHRI    r2, 8                   ; the action
        MOV16   r7, DATA
        ST      r2, r7, 2               ; LAST
        CMPI    r2, NONE
        BZ      hunt
        JMP     apply                   ; (after the data: thread 0 spills there)

; wlow / whigh: ir_nec_rx.loom's, unchanged. Wait until RX is low / high
; or TD is reached, through the glitch filter. T = 1: TD came first. T = 0:
; the new level held for r5 slots (2 ticks); a confirmed edge restarts the
; tick grid (CSRW TICK_INT clears ACC).
wlow:   WAITP   RX, 0, T
        BT      wlr
        MOV     r7, r5
wld:    DJNZ    r7, wld
        JP      RX, 1, wlow             ; high again: a glitch
        CSRW    TICK_INT, r6            ; the next window starts here
wlr:    RET
whigh:  WAITP   RX, 1, T
        BT      whr
        MOV     r7, r5
whd:    DJNZ    r7, whd
        JP      RX, 0, whigh            ; low again: a glitch
        CSRW    TICK_INT, r6
whr:    RET

; ============================================== thread 1: STATE to WS2812
; ws2812.loom's transmitter with its INQ replaced by three registers.
; r0 the bit shifter (the byte in 15:8, shifted left), r1 the bits left in
; the byte (then the reset-gap count), r2 the state last sent, r3 r4 r5 G R
; B in bits 15:8, r6 the LEDs left, r7 DATA (the .csr scratch before).
        .thread 1
        .equ    T0H  = 20               ; 0.40 us
        .equ    T1H  = 40               ; 0.80 us
        .equ    PER  = 64               ; 1.28 us
        .equ    T0L  = PER - T0H        ; 44 = 0.88 us
        .equ    T1L  = PER - T1H        ; 24 = 0.48 us
        .equ    FIRST = 32              ; re-anchor to the first rise
        .equ    RSTW = 250              ; reset gap = RSTN * RSTW clocks
        .equ    RSTN = 11               ; 2750 clocks = 55 us > 50 us
        .equ    POLL = 250              ; STATE is read every 5 us when idle

wstart: .csr    TICK_INT, 1             ; one tick is one clock: the datasheet
        .csr    TICK_FRAC, 0            ; fixes this rate, not the host
        SETP    DOUT, 0                 ; the line idles low
        MOV16   r7, DATA
        LDI     r2, 0
        NOT     r2, r2                  ; 0xFFFF: no state sent yet
        SETD    0

; Poll STATE; on a change take it whole, then build G, R and B.
poll:   LD      r0, r7, 0               ; STATE: one access, one whole word
        CMP     r0, r2
        BZ      idle
        JMP     change                  ; (after the data: thread 1 spills there)
idle:   WAITD   POLL
        JMP     poll

; A frame starts as ws2812.loom's does, with the first G byte.
lit:    SETD    0
        MOV     r0, r3
        LDI     r1, 8
        SETP    DOUT, 1, D              ; stage the first rise
        SHLI    r0, 1                   ; C = the first bit
        BC      first1
        WAITD   FIRST                   ; the rise lands on this deadline
        JMP     g_z
first1: WAITD   FIRST
        JMP     g_o

; The bit loop, once per byte of an LED (header). Each copy is
; ws2812.loom's z_bit / o_bit: entered at the rise of a bit whose value it
; is named for (_z a 0, _o a 1), TD that rise's deadline, r0 the bits not
; yet sent and r1 how many are left, this one included. At the end of a
; byte the copy loads the next byte and jumps into the next copy.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------- G byte
g_z:    SETP    DOUT, 0, D              ; stage the fall
        WAITD   T0H                     ; 0.40 us high; the fall lands here
        DJNZ    r1, g_zn
        MOV     r0, r4                  ; G done: R next
        LDI     r1, 8
        JMP     r_zn
g_zn:   SETP    DOUT, 1, D              ; stage the next rise
        SHLI    r0, 1                   ; C = the next bit
        BC      g_z1
        WAITD   T0L                     ; the rise lands on the period boundary
        JMP     g_z
g_z1:   WAITD   T0L
        JMP     g_o
g_o:    SETP    DOUT, 0, D              ; stage the fall
        DJNZ    r1, g_on
        MOV     r0, r4
        LDI     r1, 8
        JMP     r_on
g_on:   WAITD   T1H                     ; 0.80 us high; the fall lands here
        SETP    DOUT, 1, D
        SHLI    r0, 1
        BC      g_o1
        WAITD   T1L
        JMP     g_z
g_o1:   WAITD   T1L
        JMP     g_o

; ---------------------------------------------------------------- R byte
r_z:    SETP    DOUT, 0, D
        WAITD   T0H
        DJNZ    r1, r_zn
        MOV     r0, r5                  ; R done: B next
        LDI     r1, 8
        JMP     b_zn
r_zn:   SETP    DOUT, 1, D
        SHLI    r0, 1
        BC      r_z1
        WAITD   T0L
        JMP     r_z
r_z1:   WAITD   T0L
        JMP     r_o
r_o:    SETP    DOUT, 0, D
        DJNZ    r1, r_on
        MOV     r0, r5
        LDI     r1, 8
        JMP     b_on
r_on:   WAITD   T1H
        SETP    DOUT, 1, D
        SHLI    r0, 1
        BC      r_o1
        WAITD   T1L
        JMP     r_z
r_o1:   WAITD   T1L
        JMP     r_o

; ---------------------------------------------------------------- B byte
; The end of a B byte is the end of an LED: the next LED's G, or the end.
b_z:    SETP    DOUT, 0, D
        WAITD   T0H
        DJNZ    r1, b_zn
        DJNZ    r6, b_zm                ; another LED?
        JMP     fend                    ; no: the fall that just landed was the last
b_zm:   MOV     r0, r3
        LDI     r1, 8
        JMP     g_zn
b_zn:   SETP    DOUT, 1, D
        SHLI    r0, 1
        BC      b_z1
        WAITD   T0L
        JMP     b_z
b_z1:   WAITD   T0L
        JMP     b_o
b_o:    SETP    DOUT, 0, D
        DJNZ    r1, b_on
        DJNZ    r6, b_om
        JMP     o_last
b_om:   MOV     r0, r3
        LDI     r1, 8
        JMP     g_on
b_on:   WAITD   T1H
        SETP    DOUT, 1, D
        SHLI    r0, 1
        BC      b_o1
        WAITD   T1L
        JMP     b_z
b_o1:   WAITD   T1L
        JMP     b_o

o_last: WAITD   T1H                     ; the fall of the frame's last bit
fend:   LDI     r1, RSTN                ; the reset gap: the strip latches
rst:    WAITD   RSTW
        DJNZ    r1, rst
        JMP     poll

; ================================================================== data
; At DATA, in thread 2's quarter. STATE is written by thread 0 only and
; read by thread 1 only; NLEDS by the host only; LAST by thread 0 only.
        .org    DATA
        .word   0x40                    ; STATE: off, colour 0, level 4
        .word   60                      ; NLEDS
        .word   NONE                    ; LAST
; The colours, G, R, B at level 7 (the wire order of a WS2812B).
        .word   255, 255, 255           ; 0 white
        .word     0, 255,   0           ; 1 red
        .word    96, 255,   0           ; 2 orange
        .word   224, 255,   0           ; 3 yellow
        .word   255,   0,   0           ; 4 green
        .word   255,   0, 255           ; 5 cyan
        .word     0,   0, 255           ; 6 blue
        .word     0, 128, 255           ; 7 violet
        .word     0, 255, 160           ; 8 magenta
        .word   160, 255,  64           ; 9 warm white
; The key map: action << 8 | NEC command.
        .word   0 << 8 | 0x16, 1 << 8 | 0x0C, 2 << 8 | 0x18, 3 << 8 | 0x5E
        .word   4 << 8 | 0x08, 5 << 8 | 0x1C, 6 << 8 | 0x5A, 7 << 8 | 0x42
        .word   8 << 8 | 0x52, 9 << 8 | 0x4A
        .word   UP << 8 | 0x15, DOWN << 8 | 0x07, POWER << 8 | 0x43

; ============================================ thread 0, continued: apply
; Past the data, in thread 2's quarter: thread 0's 128 words hold the
; receiver and the key lookup, and this is the rest.
        .thread 0
        .org    KEYS + NKEYS
; Apply action r2 to STATE (r7 = DATA). Only this thread writes STATE.
apply:  LD      r3, r7, 0               ; STATE
        CMPI    r2, UP
        BC      notcol
        SHRI    r3, 4                   ; a colour: bits 3:0 = r2, and on
        SHLI    r3, 4
        OR      r3, r3, r2
        LDI     r4, ON
        OR      r3, r3, r4
        JMP     store
notcol: LDI     r4, ON
        CMPI    r2, POWER
        BNZ     level
        XOR     r3, r3, r4              ; on / off
        JMP     store
level:  TEST    r3, r4
        BZ      same                    ; off: VOL+ and VOL- do nothing
        MOV     r4, r3
        SHRI    r4, 4
        ANDI    r4, 7                   ; the level
        CMPI    r2, UP
        BNZ     down
        CMPI    r4, 7
        BZ      same                    ; already the brightest
        ADDI    r3, 16
        JMP     store
down:   TEST    r4, r4
        BZ      same                    ; already the dimmest
        SUBI    r3, 16
store:  ST      r3, r7, 0               ; one access: never half written
same:   JMP     hunt                    ; (out of a branch's reach)
t0end:                                  ; the first word after thread 0

; ============================================ thread 1, continued: change
; Also past the data: STATE has changed, so build G, R and B from it.
        .thread 1
        .org    t0end
change: MOV     r2, r0                  ; the state this frame shows
        LD      r6, r7, 1               ; NLEDS
        MOV     r1, r0
        ANDI    r1, 15                  ; the colour c
        MOV     r5, r1
        SHLI    r5, 1
        ADD     r5, r5, r1
        ADD     r5, r5, r7              ; DATA + 3c
        LD      r3, r5, COLS - DATA     ; G, R, B in bits 7:0
        LD      r4, r5, COLS - DATA + 1
        LD      r5, r5, COLS - DATA + 2
        MOV     r1, r0
        SHRI    r1, 4
        ANDI    r1, 7                   ; the level L
        LDI     r0, 7
        SUB     r1, r0, r1              ; 7 - L
        SHR     r3, r3, r1              ; c >> (7 - L)
        SWAP    r3, r3                  ; to bits 15:8, MSB first
        SHR     r4, r4, r1
        SWAP    r4, r4
        SHR     r5, r5, r1
        SWAP    r5, r5
        MOV     r0, r2
        SHRI    r0, 7                   ; the ON bit (STATE < 0x100)
        BNZ     go
        LDI     r3, 0                   ; off: every LED dark
        LDI     r4, 0
        LDI     r5, 0
go:     JMP     lit
